tive = headwind from that axis)\n- Interest rate sensitivity: \u22121 (long-duration biotech cash flows; offset by self-funding balance sheet)\n- Dollar sensitivity: \u22121 (US-centric revenue; international expansion deprioritized)\n- Liquidity sensitivity: \u22121 (no external capital needed; pipeline funded internally)\n- Economic cycle sensitivity: \u22122 (chronic disease = defensive but not anti-cyclic)\n- Commodity sensitivity: 0 (negligible input costs)\n- Weighted macro impact: \u22120.4 \u2248 Neutral/slight headwind\n\nStep 1-4. Macro Alignment Judgment: NEUTRAL \u2014 no conviction adjustment.\nHealthcare defensiveness offsets long-duration valuation risk from 4.7% 10Y.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 1.5. BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nStep 1.5-1. Base Rate Inquiry\n- Quality compounder (3+ marketed products, positive FCF): 5-year survival rate \u2248 90%+ [Assumption \u2014 reference base rate from broad pharma universe]\n- Phase 3 schizophrenia novel mechanism success: \u2248 40% [Assumption \u2014 post-Karuna/Cobenfy class validation raises the muscarinic base rate; typical Phase 3 CNS rate 30\u201350%]\n- Phase 3 MDD success: \u2248 35% [Assumption \u2014 historical CNS/MDD Phase 3 rates]\n- New rare-disease launch with 7 patient deaths + 100+ SAEs in FAERS within first year: probability of material label restriction or withdrawal \u2248 30\u201340% [Estimate \u2014 base rates from FDA safety actions on novel orphan drugs]\n- Large M&A (>$2B) achieving ROIC > WACC: \u2248 25\u201335% [Assumption \u2014 per framework reference table]\n\nStep 1.5-2. Deviation from Base Rate (justification required)\nMy Base-Case valuation assumes VYKAT survives with modest growth (not blockbuster). That is LESS optimistic than management but CONSISTENT with the safety-signal base rate. My Bull case (20% probability) is above the raw base rate for \"both Phase 3s succeed + VYKAT clears\" \u2014 deviating because: (1) the M4 mechanism already validated by Cobenfey in the same indication; (2) osavampator has a differentiated mechanism with Phase 2b signal; (3) Neurocrine's 20-year CNS development track record. Justification judged ADEQUATE.\n\nStep 1.5-3. Base Rate Shock Check\n- Success probability (combined bull + rich base) \u2248 70% vs \"both Phase 3s succeed\" base rate \u2248 15% = 4.7\u00d7 \u2192 RED FLAG. Rebalanced: Bull only prices pipeline optionality, not core survival. Core success base rate (revenue survival) is high, so no violation. Noted in Audit.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY (BUFFETT + LYNCH)\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nStep 2-1. Business Understanding (3 sentences)\nNeurocrine is a neuroscience-focused biopharmaceutical company with three commercial products: INGREZZA (valbenazine, a VMAT2 inhibitor for tardive dyskinesia and Huntington's chorea), CRENESSITY (crinecerfont, a CRF1 antagonist for classic congenital adrenal hyperplasia), and VYKAT XR (diazoxide choline, acquired via Soleno, for hyperphagia in Prader-Willi syndrome). The company also runs a deep episodic CNS pipeline anchored by two Phase 3 programs \u2014 osavampator in major depressive disorder and direclidine (NBI-1117568) in schizophrenia \u2014 both expected to read out in 2027. It is rare among mid-cap biotechs: GAAP-profitable, self-funding, with ~$0.87B TTM FCF.\nUnderstandability: HIGH.\n\nStep 2-2. Moat Assessment + Dynamics\n| Moat Type | Score (0\u20133) | Direction | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Intangible assets | 3 | Stable | INGREZZA brand + data; head-to-head occupancy data vs Austedo [Actual \u2014 FY2025 PR] |\n| Switching costs | 2 | Stable | Prescriber inertia; payer contracts (70% Medicare lives covered) [Actual \u2014 Q2 call] |\n| Network effects | 0 | n/a | None |\n| Cost advantage | 1 | Stable | No scale advantage; royalty-bearing products |\n| Economies of scale | 2 | Widening | 3 launches amortize SG&A; sales force expansion 2026 |\n| Regulatory barriers | 3 | Narrows for INGREZZA (ODE expires Aug 2030), Wide for CRENESSITY/VYKAT orphan status | [Actual \u2014 Pharsight] |\n| Data moat | 1 | Stable | Real-world CNS registry data |\n| TOTAL | 12 / 21 | Stable-to-Widening | WIDE (low end) |\n\nStep 2-3. Lynch Classification\n- Classification: FAST GROWER (revenue CAGR 22.3%, 5-year) transitioning toward STALWART (growth expected to decelerate as base grows and INGREZZA matures).\n- Checkpoints: (1) Revenue growth vs earnings growth \u2014 EPS grew faster (+17% CAGR vs revenue +22% historically; 2025 EPS +42%); (2) Debt: none [Actual \u2014 Q2 call]; (3) Institutional sponsorship: 105.8% (crowded but stable).\n\nStep 2-4. Financial Strength Scorecard\n- Revenue growth (3-yr CAGR): +24.4% [Arithmetic \u2014 $1.489B 2022 \u2192 $2.861B 2025]\n- Revenue growth (TTM): +39.5% YoY in Q2 2026 [Actual]\n- Operating margin (FY2025): 21.6% GAAP [Actual]; non-GAAP ~29.3% [Estimate]\n- ROE (FY2025): 22.1% [Actual \u2014 vendor]\n- ROIC (GAAP, FY2025): \u2248 43% [Estimate \u2014 NOPAT $0.489B / avg IC ~$1.1B; IC distorted by high cash]\n- FCF margin: 26.2% of revenue FY2025 [Arithmetic]\n- FCF conversion: OCF/NI = 1.63\u00d7 [Arithmetic]; FCF/OCF = 0.96\u00d7 [Arithmetic]\n- Net debt: net cash \u2248 $0.5B [Estimate]\n- Piotroski F-score: 5/7 available checks [Arithmetic]; accruals ratio \u22120.073 (favourable) [Arithmetic]\nJudgment: EXCELLENT.\n\nStep 2-5. Capital Allocation Scorecard (0\u201315)\n1. Buyback efficiency: 1/3 \u2014 net issuance \u22121.2%, but SBC offsets most buyback benefit [Arithmetic]\n2. M&A track record: 2/3 \u2014 Soleno ($2.9B) has logical strategic fit; earliest returns unclear\n3. Capex efficiency: 3/3 \u2014 capex only ~1% of revenue; incremental ROIC extremely high [Arithmetic]\n4. Rationality of mix: 2/3 \u2014 heavy R&D reinvestment appropriate; but 35% of revenue R&D is a deliberate growth bet\n5. Guidance accuracy: 3/3 \u2014 raised 2026 INGREZZA guidance mid-year; non-GAAP EPS beat by $0.24 in Q1 [Actual]\nInsider adjustments: no CEO >5% holding; no cluster insider buys \u2192 no bonus/penalty\nTOTAL: 11/15 (GOOD)\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 2.5. INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nStep 2.5-1. R&D Capitalization\n- 2025 GAAP R&D: $1.016B [Actual]; 2024: $0.731B [Actual]; 2023: \u2248$0.691B [Estimate derived from Rev \u2212 COGS \u2212 SG&A \u2212 OpInc]; 2022: \u2248$0.442B [Estimate]\n- 4-yr amortization \u2248 $0.720B [Assumption]\n- Adjusted operating income = $0.619B + $1.016B \u2212 $0.720B = $0.915B [Estimate]\n\nStep 2.5-2. SBC Treatment\n- FY2025 SBC: $0.218B [Actual]. Adjusted owner FCF = OCF \u2212 capex \u2212 SBC = $0.783 \u2212 $0.034 \u2212 $0.218 = $0.531B [Arithmetic]. SBC is NOT added back in this analysis.\n\nStep 2.5-3. Operating Lease Capitalization\n- FY2025 operating leases $0.471B present value [Actual \u2014 Section 6B]. Minor; not added to net debt because the company holds $2.5B liquidity and no debt.\n\nStep 2.5-4. Brand/Data Asset Valuation\n- Not quantified \u2014 niche CNS brands have less transferable brand value. [Unconfirmed] \u2014 no reliable Interbrand-style figure for NBIX brands.\n\nStep 2.5-5. Adjusted ROIC\n- Adjusted NOPAT: $0.915B \u00d7 0.79 = $0.723B [Estimate]\n- Adjusted invested capital: reported IC \u2248 $1.125B (equity $3.253B + debt $0.415B \u2212 cash/investments $2.543B) + unamortized R&D \u2248 $2.16B = $3.285B\n- Adjusted ROIC \u2248 22.0% [Estimate]; GAAP ROIC \u2248 43% [Estimate]; gap >5pp explained by R&D expensing and cash distortion. Both confirm HIGH quality.\n\nStep 2.5-6. Owner Earnings (Buffett)\nOwner Earnings (2025) = NI $0.479B + D&A $0.030B \u2212 maintenance capex $0.034B \u2212 \u0394WC (\u2212$0.015B) = $0.490B [Estimate]\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 3. VALUATION \u2014 ENGINE 1 (DCF + MULTIPLES)\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nStep 0-1/0-2. Archetype & Engine Selection\n- Archetype: B \u2014 Quality Compounder\n  (1) 3 consecutive years of GAAP profitability; (2) FCF margin ~26%; (3) ROIC > WACC for 5+ years; (4) revenue growth 20%+ but with durable products, not option value).\n- Engine 1 (Traditional DCF + Multiples).\n- If Engine 4 (Option-Led) were used instead, it would over-weight the 2027 Phase 3 binaries and under-weight the already-commercial base \u2014 base rates for Phase 3 success (~35\u201340%) do not justify treating pipeline as core value.\n- Not in transition; all criteria for Quality Compounder met.\n\nStep 3-1. WACC Derivation\n- Rf = 4.70% [Actual \u2014 FRED 10Y 2026-08-21]\n- ERP = 4.75% [Assumption \u2014 long-run historical/implied range]\n- Beta = 0.85 [Assumption \u2014 vendor 0.394 is on an unstated window and is inconsistent with a mid-cap growth biopharma; normalized 0.75\u20131.0. Unlevered pharma base rate ~0.65, levered with pipeline optionality \u2192 0.85]\n- Cost of equity = 4.70% + 0.85 \u00d7 4.75% = 8.74%\n- WACC = 8.74% \u2248 8.7% (all-equity; no debt, net cash addressed at equity bridge)\n\nStep 3-2. Explicit Forecast (all $B; fiscal years)\n| FY | Revenue | OpMargin | EBIT | NOPAT(21%) | D&A | Capex | \u0394WC | Owner FCF |\n|----|---------|----------|------|-----------|-----|-------|-----|-----------|\n| 2026E | 3.85 | 29.1% | 1.121 | 0.886 | 0.100 | 0.050 | 0.150 | 0.786 |\n| 2027E | 4.58 | 30.5% | 1.397 | 1.104 | 0.120 | 0.060 | 0.110 | 1.054 |\n| 2028E | 5.22 | 31.0% | 1.618 | 1.278 | 0.150 | 0.065 | 0.096 | 1.267 |\n| 2029E | 5.04 | 30.0% | 1.512 | 1.194 | 0.180 | 0.070 | \u22120.027 | 1.331 |\n| 2030E | 4.86 | 29.0% | 1.409 | 1.113 | 0.200 | 0.075 | \u22120.027 | 1.265 |\n| 2031E | 4.36 | 28.5% | 1.243 | 0.982 | 0.220 | 0.080 | \u22120.075 | 1.197 |\n| 2032E | 3.83 | 28.0% | 1.072 | 0.847 | 0.240 | 0.085 | \u22120.079 | 1.081 |\nTerminal: g = 2.5% [Assumption \u2014 long-run nominal GDP]; Terminal FCF = 1.081\u00d71.025 = 1.108; TV = 1.108/(0.087\u22120.025) = $17.87B.\n\nFORECAST SOURCES:\n- 2026 revenue: $3.85B = INGREZZA guidance mid $2.85B [Actual \u2014 Q2 call raised guidance] + CRENESSITY ~$0.73B [Estimate \u2014 Q2 $184M annualizing >$735M; Q1 ~$131M] + VYKAT recognized ~$0.24B [Estimate \u2014 $54M Q2, slower H2 due to discontinuations] + other $0.045B [Actual run-rate]\n- INGREZZA 2027 +10%, 2028 +7% [Assumption \u2014 mgmt: \"continued growth through 2027-2028\"; VMAT2 class growing double-digit]\n- INGREZZA 2029 \u221215% [Assumption \u2014 IRA negotiation implementation 2029 per mgmt; Medicare-heavy TD population]\n- INGREZZA 2030 \u221215%, 2031 \u221230%, 2032 \u221240% [Assumption \u2014 core compound patent expiries 2031\u20132037 (Pharsight); generic valbenazine approved but settlement-limited]\n- CRENESSITY 2027 $1.0B \u2192 2032 $1.80B [Assumption \u2014 Q2 annualized $735M at 15% penetration; 40% penetration by 2032]\n- VYKAT 2027 $0.40B \u2192 2032 $0.95B [Assumption \u2014 blockbuster potential per mgmt; slowed by STAT safety signal (7 deaths, 100+ SAEs unadjudicated)]\n- Op margins 29\u201331% 2026-28, declining to 28% by 2032 [Assumption \u2014 guidance R&D mid $1.225B and SG&A mid $1.388B for 2026 [Actual]; operating leverage then